Subject: Store a small array literal's elements in the array's own block

What this does
  The register VM builds an array literal of up to 16 elements as one
  allocation: the array object, its inline property slots, and the element
  storage right after them. A new `values_inline` byte, in padding the object
  already had, records that the element storage is not a separate block.
  Growing such an array copies its elements to a heap buffer and clears the
  byte; the finalizer and the fast-to-slow array conversion free the storage
  only when it is a separate block.

Why we need it
  Each array literal was two allocations and two frees, plus a growth call
  that sized the second one. On Octane splay, which builds a ten-element
  array for every payload node, creating the array cost about 700
  instructions, and freeing it touched a second cache line per array.

What it adds
  JSObject.values_inline           - the element storage lives in the object's
                                     own block.
  JS_NewObjectFromShapeCapExtra()  - object creation with extra element slots
                                     reserved in the same block.
  JS_ARRAY_INLINE_MAX              - the largest literal stored this way.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

diff --git a/quickjs.c b/quickjs.c
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -2079,6 +2079,7 @@
                                     11 flag bits + 5 = exactly the 2-byte slot
